{"rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.", "text": "The headlights stayed where they were for nearly an hour. Mira watched them through the streaming glass while Tobias returned to the clockwork, winding it with a care that seemed almost reverent. Every so often the beam swept across the distant car, and each time Mira imagined a face behind the windshield, pale and upturned, waiting. Then, a little after one o'clock, the headlights went dark. \"They've given up,\" Tobias said, sounding relieved. Mira didn't answer. She was watching the coast road, and in the brief white flare of the next sweep she saw a figure walking along the verge, head bowed against the rain, coming steadily toward the tower. It carried no torch. It did not hurry. It moved as though the path were perfectly familiar, as though it had walked this way many times before. \"Tobias,\" she whispered. He came to stand beside her, and together they watched the figure cross the causeway, vanishing and reappearing with each turn of the lens. When it reached the foot of the tower, it stopped and looked up. Mira could not see its face, only the shape of it, still as a stone against the churning dark. Somewhere far below, the heavy iron door groaned on its hinges. Neither of them had unlocked it. Tobias reached for her hand, and she let him take it. On the desk behind them, the logbook lay open to a fresh page, and in the lamplight Mira saw that the first line had already been written, in a hand that was not her own. Below, the footsteps began to climb."}
